      "body": "\n## Purpose\n\nReplace a per-dispatch `torch.cuda.get_device_capability()` call with the cached `current_platform` already used elsewhere in the file.\n\nReverted the static-grid/lambda-to-tuple change: it doesn't implement true static-grid (SM-count-independent) launch, and risks going stale if `TILE_SIZE` is ever autotuned. Kept the platform-checker fix only.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n```bash\npytest -sv tests/v1/test_kv_cache_utils.py\n```\n\n---\n<details>\n<summary> Essential Elements of an Effective PR Description Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] The purpose of the PR, such as \"Fix some issue (link existing issues this PR will resolve)\".\n- [x] The test plan, such as providing test command.\n- [x] The test results, such as pasting the results comparison before and after, or e2e results\n- [ ] (Optional) The necessary documentation update, such as updating `supported_models.md` and `examples` for a new model.\n</details>\n",
      "changedFiles": 1,
      "comments": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "namgyu-yun"
            },
            "body": "cc @MatthewBonanni Could you please take a look at this? It replaces a per-dispatch `torch.cuda.get_device_capability()` call in `triton_reshape_and_cache_flash.py` with the cached `current_platform` check already used in the file.",
            "createdAt": "2026-07-06T07:27:33Z",
            "databaseId": 4890183860,
            "updatedAt": "2026-07-06T07:27:33Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46060#issuecomment-4890183860"
          },
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "namgyu-yun"
            },
            "body": "@bringlein could you please look into this PR? It addresses TODO based on your mark.",
            "createdAt": "2026-08-04T02:26:21Z",
            "databaseId": 5173893690,
            "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T02:26:21Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46060#issuecomment-5173893690"
          },
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "bringlein"
            },
            "body": "Hey @namgyu-youn , thank you for your contribution! \r\n\r\nWhile the first change makes sense, the replacement of the launch-grid calculation does not, I'm afraid. \r\n\"Static launch grid\" means here a technique where the launch grid size equals the number of SMs and does not scale with the workload size. That's the \"static\" part. It requires a redesign of the kernel because then each kernel instance needs to figure out how much work needs to be done by this instance (since the launch grid does _not_ scale with the workload, hence the kernel need to be adaptive). \r\nThis technique is known to improve performance but is quite complicated...and I'm not sure any more that for the small kernel of `reshape_and_cache` it would make sense, since I won't expect it to be noticed end-to-end (but I'm happy to be proven wrong!). \r\n\r\nThe replacement of the lambda with a pre-computed tuple doesn't change the overhead, it just moves it a few lines earlier. Also, it risks to make the result incorrect because sometimes `TILE_SIZE` is determined by the autotuner or heuristics later. \r\n\r\nMaybe another improvement worth for this small kernel might be tensor descriptors, see #42545. But also there, the reshape_and_cache has ~<4% runtime end-to-end, so an improvement might not be noticed end-to-end. ",
            "createdAt": "2026-08-04T10:01:16Z",
            "databaseId": 5177467899,
            "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T10:01:16Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/46060#issuecomment-5177467899"
          },
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "namgyu-yun"
            },
            "body": "> Hey @namgyu-youn , thank you for your contribution!\r\n> \r\n> While the first change makes sense, the replacement of the launch-grid calculation does not, I'm afraid. \"Static launch grid\" means here a technique where the launch grid size equals the number of SMs and does not scale with the workload size. That's the \"static\" part. It requires a redesign of the kernel because then each kernel instance needs to figure out how much work needs to be done by this instance (since the launch grid does _not_ scale with the workload, hence the kernel need to be adaptive). This technique is known to improve performance but is quite complicated...and I'm not sure any more that for the small kernel of `reshape_and_cache` it would make sense, since I won't expect it to be noticed end-to-end (but I'm happy to be proven wrong!).\r\n\r\n@bringlein thanks so much for the detailed review. I didn't know those details, and didn't think about them.\r\n\r\n> The replacement of the lambda with a pre-computed tuple doesn't change the overhead, it just moves it a few lines earlier. Also, it risks to make the result incorrect because sometimes `TILE_SIZE` is determined by the autotuner or heuristics later.\r\n> \r\n> Maybe another improvement worth for this small kernel might be tensor descriptors, see #42545. But also there, the reshape_and_cache has ~<4% runtime end-to-end, so an improvement might not be noticed end-to-end.\r\n\r\nYeah mostly agree with you. I was also wondering if this update would derive meaningful perf enhancement, and wasn't sure about it.\r\n\r\nSo in my thought, reverting lambda would be a option. I updated this PR too, could you look into it? Let me know if this makes sense.",
